[Q]: Write a Perl script that reads a CSV file with headers and outputs the data in JSON format.
[A]: Here's a Perl script that does exactly what you've asked:

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Text::CSV;
use JSON;

my $csv_file = 'data.csv';
my $json_file = 'output.json';

my $csv = Text::CSV->new({ binary => 1 });
open(my $fh_in, '<', $csv_file) or die "Can't open $csv_file: $!";
open(my $fh_out, '>', $json_file) or die "Can't open $json_file: $!";

print $fh_out JSON::encode({}, { pretty => 1 }), "\n";

while (my $row = $csv->getline($fh_in)) {
    my %data;
    for (my $i = 0; $i < $csv->columns(); $i++) {
        $data{ $csv->column_names()->[$i] } = $row->[$i];
    }
    print $fh_out JSON::encode(\%data), "\n";
}

close($fh_in);
close($fh_out);
```

Assuming your CSV file is named `data.csv` and has headers, this script will read the file, convert each row into a hash with the column names as keys, and output the resulting JSON data to a file called `output.json`. You can customize the JSON encoding options by passing them as arguments to `JSON::encode()`. The script also prints the entire JSON object as output for better readability.

