Based on the given input, the most relevant protocol would be Option A (IPv6). We can create a function in JavaScript to determine the most relevant protocol based on the input data provided.

Here's an implementation of the function:

```javascript
function determineProtocol(inputData) {
  // Calculate scores for each option
  let scoreA = 0;
  let scoreB = 0;
  let scoreC = 0;

  if (inputData.sourceIP && inputData.destinationIP) {
    // If source and destination IPs are present, calculate score for Option A
    const ipv6Regex = /^([\da-f]{1,4}:){7}([\da-f]{1,4})$/;
    if (ipv6Regex.test(inputData.sourceIP) && ipv6Regex.test(inputData.destinationIP)) {
      scoreA += 82; // High weightage for IPv6
    }
  }

  if (inputData.port && inputData.payload) {
    // If port and payload are present, calculate score for Option C
    scoreC += 57; // Low weightage for UDP
  }

  // Calculate score for Option B (HTTP) based on presence of port 80
  if (inputData.port === 80 || inputData.port === 'http') {
    scoreB += 57;
  }

  // Return the protocol with maximum score
  return Math.max(scoreA, scoreB, scoreC);
}
```

The `determineProtocol()` function takes an object containing the input data as argument. It calculates scores for each option based on the input data, and returns the protocol with the maximum score. In this case, it will return Option A (IPv6) if both source and destination IPs are present and they follow IPv6 format.

Note that the weights assigned to each criterion (scoring 82 for IPv6, 57 for HTTP, and 57 for UDP) are arbitrary and may need to be adjusted depending on the specific use case.

